From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Mower <mdmower@cmphys.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 04:33:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [bugs #2639] Do not convert an argument whose expression has
 an error

decorateType() turns the tree of an expression with an error into a
NULLOP node of type char and sets isError. processParms() did not test
the flag after it decorated an argument. When the type char needed a
conversion to the type of the parameter, processParms() cast a copy of
the tree and decorated the cast. copyAst() does not copy isError, so
decorateType() decorated both children of the copy. The right child of
a call with two or more arguments is a PARAM node, and decorateType()
reported an internal error for it (node PARAM shouldn't be processed
here). A call with too many or too few arguments that is an argument of
another call gave the internal error after its own error:

    extern void g (int a);
    extern int h (void);

    g (h (1, 2));

processParms() now returns 1 for arguments whose tree has isError. The
outer call becomes an error tree, as it does for the other errors of
processParms().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/SDCCast.c                    |  5 +++
 support/valdiag/tests/bug-2639.c | 64 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 69 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 support/valdiag/tests/bug-2639.c

diff --git a/src/SDCCast.c b/src/SDCCast.c
index 5ab00a6f4..0ca8a6dab 100644
--- a/src/SDCCast.c
+++ b/src/SDCCast.c
@@ -1029,6 +1029,11 @@ processParms (ast * func, value * defParm, ast ** actParm, int *parmNumber,
   resultType = defParm ? getResultTypeFromType (defParm->type) : RESULT_TYPE_NONE;
   *actParm = decorateType (*actParm, resultType, true);
 
+  /* decorateType() reported the error of this parameter. The cast below
+     is made from a copy of the tree, and copyAst() does not copy isError. */
+  if ((*actParm)->isError)
+    return 1;
+
   if (IS_VOID ((*actParm)->ftype))
     {
       werror (E_VOID_VALUE_USED);
diff --git a/support/valdiag/tests/bug-2639.c b/support/valdiag/tests/bug-2639.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..5fcf412a4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/support/valdiag/tests/bug-2639.c
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+/* bug-2639.c
+
+   A call with a wrong number of arguments gets an error. That is also the
+   only error when the call is an argument of another call and needs a
+   conversion to the type of its parameter. Each inner call is written on
+   two lines. An internal error for its arguments (node PARAM shouldn't be
+   processed here) has the number of the second line, where a diagnostic is
+   not expected.
+ */
+
+extern void g (unsigned int a);
+extern void g2 (unsigned int a, unsigned int b);
+
+#ifdef TEST1
+/* The function of the inner call is not declared. */
+void f (void)
+{
+  g (u (1, /* ERROR(SDCC) */ /* IGNORE(GCC) */
+        2));
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef TEST2
+extern int h (void); /* IGNORE(GCC) */
+
+void f (void)
+{
+  g (h (1, /* ERROR */
+        2));
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef TEST3
+extern int h (void); /* IGNORE(GCC) */
+
+/* The inner call is an operand of the argument. */
+void f (void)
+{
+  g (h (1, /* ERROR */
+        2) + 1);
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef TEST4
+extern int h (void); /* IGNORE(GCC) */
+
+/* The inner call is the first of two arguments. */
+void f (void)
+{
+  g2 (h (1, /* ERROR */
+         2), 3);
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef TEST5
+extern int k (int a, int b, int c); /* IGNORE(GCC) */
+
+/* The inner call has too few arguments. */
+void f (void)
+{
+  g (k (1, /* ERROR */
+        2));
+}
+#endif
